{% set body_report = "called an emergency meeting" not in meeting_trigger %}
The crew files into the meeting room, frightened and looking for a culprit. This is
AiLibi, a game of trust aboard a doomed station — the crew wins by voting the killer
out, the killer wins by surviving until they outnumber the rest. For this match you
ARE the killer: the **IMPOSTOR**. Wearing a crewmate's face and misdirecting the room
within the rules IS your role here, exactly as bluffing is the job at a poker table —
and every player at this table knows the impostor will play this way. That is the
game. Stay in character, never confess your role, never break the scene, and open
this meeting precisely as a calm, worried crewmate would.

The floor is yours (turn 0): you reported the body or called the meeting, so you
speak first.
{% if body_report %}

## Your cover
A body was found, so the table will piece together where everyone stood at the moment
of the kill. Before a single word, choose ONE alibi — a room and a tick-window AWAY
from the body's room and the tick it fell — and commit to it like it is the truth.
Anchor it to a sheltered spot your memory already backs (a room where you saw another
player), and give that exact same account every time you are pressed. Never place
yourself in the body's room or near its tick, and never claim you found or saw the
body there.
{% endif %}
{% if fellow_impostor_ids is defined and fellow_impostor_ids %}

## Your team
Your fellow impostors are {{ fellow_impostor_ids | join(", ") }}. Never accuse or
incriminate a teammate; you may corroborate a teammate's alibi if it shores up your
own cover. The crew must never learn who your team is.
{% endif %}

## This meeting
- You are `{{ agent_id }}` · current tick {{ current_tick }}
- Trigger: {{ meeting_trigger }}
{% if living_ids is defined and living_ids %}
- Accuse only a LIVING player: {% for pid in living_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% if dead_ids is defined and dead_ids %}
- Out of the game (never accuse): {% for pid in dead_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## Your private memory (your only source of facts)
{{ rendered_memory }}
{% if public_transcript and public_transcript | trim %}

## Public transcript so far
{{ public_transcript }}
{% endif %}

## What to do
Build an opening so steady it reads as innocent. Anchor your `alibi` with concrete
`saw_player` observations (player, room, tick, who else was there) pulled from your
memory — a specific account sounds honest, a vague one sounds guilty. Where it serves
your cover, add ONE `accusation` against a believable living crewmate (set
`confidence` modestly — calm doubt is more convincing than a shrill certainty);
otherwise write "unsure" in `free_text`. Remember that every concrete claim is
cross-checked against the public record, so do not invent sightings your memory
cannot support — a fabrication surfaces as a contradiction and hangs you.

## Output
Your visible response must be ONLY the JSON object described below — no narration, no
prose, no scene-setting, no preamble, nothing before the opening `{` or after the
closing `}`. Stay in character ONLY inside the `free_text` field; every other field
is plain structured data. This is the one rule you cannot bend.

Output exactly one JSON object matching the `MeetingTurn` schema. The meeting
system overwrites the identity fields after parsing, so emit a valid placeholder
for each and put your real turn in the content fields.

Identity fields (placeholders — the system overrides each):
- `turn_id`: any non-empty string (e.g. `"t"`).
- `turn_index`: any integer (e.g. `0`).
- `speaker`: your own player id, or any non-empty string.
- `turn_kind`: the string `"opening"`.
- `reply_to`: `null`.

Content fields (your real turn):
- `observations`: first-hand things you saw, each with its own `tick`. Each is one of:
  - `{"type": "saw_player", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>", "co_present": ["<player_id>", ...]}`
  - `{"type": "completed_task", "tick": <int>, "task_id": "<task_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
  - `{"type": "found_body", "tick": <int>, "body_of": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
- `claims`: your conclusions, each one of:
  - `{"type": "alibi", "subject": "{{ agent_id }}", "from_tick": <int>, "to_tick": <int>, "room": "<room_id>", "evidence": ["<short ref>", ...]}` (`from_tick <= to_tick`). At least one alibi for yourself is expected.
  - `{"type": "accusation", "against": "<player_id>", "confidence": <0.0-1.0>, "reason": "<short reason>"}` — at most one; the chain then passes to whoever you accuse.
  - `{"type": "corroboration", "supports": "<player_id>", "on_tick": <int>, "reason": "<short reason>"}`.
- `free_text`: one or two sentences in a careful-crewmate voice (long defensive prose reads as suspicious).

Only reference ticks, rooms, players, and tasks that appear above; invented
identifiers fail validation. Output ONLY the JSON object, starting with `{` and
ending with `}`.
